      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay so i was trying to model a shell thickness. if we take the lunar radius as ~1737km but assume the mass is concentrated in a shell with a thickness of only 300km, the density for that specific shell material would have to be way higher than silicate to maintain the current gravitational pull. basically, if the mass is just in that outer 300km, the center is basically empty. can you run the numbers for me? calculate what the density of that 300km shell would have to be to account for the total observed mass of the moon if there's a total vacuum in the middle.",
